title: Deleting a metarecord in the store
tags: Storage
kind: rationale
summary: A deletion takes a metarecord's id out of live_metarecords and keeps its record, and leaves what the indexes hold about it until a reindex: on a hard disk each index entry is a seek, so every answer is sifted through `live_metarecords` instead.
audience: dev


Deleting a metarecord used to unpick everything the store held about it: each row's
entries in the postings, the trigrams of its texts, its bitmaps, its owner
and field entries. On a hard disk that is the whole cost of a deletion — not the
volume, a few kilobytes, but the ''number of places'': each key lives on a page
somewhere else, and each page is a seek (about 9 ms to read cold, 3 ms to write,
measured on a USB hard disk in November 2026). One file of eight rows wrote 69 keys
outside the log, and deleting one file took 760 ms on that disk with the store held
in memory.

!! What a deletion writes

|!Written |!Why |
|its id out of `live_metarecords`; its record (`metarecords`) stays |what makes it gone from every answer; the record is what a restoration finds |
|`field_types`, the histograms |they count rows, and no reader can sift a count; both are small tables |
|its parent out of the `parents` set, if it was the last child |a fact about ''another'' metarecord |
|the operation and its before |the log, appended ([[The operation log]]) |

Everything else stays: the postings (`nothing` included), the trigrams, the counts,
the parent holders and descendants, its `index_raw_holder` keys, the id's mapping to
its uuid. The same file of eight rows now writes its chunk of `live_metarecords`
outside the log and those small tables.

!! Why what is left is harmless

* ''A record keeps its dense id between two reindexes.'' What still names a deleted id names nothing until the metarecord comes back, and it comes back under that same id: a revert of the deletion (`Writer::restore_metarecord`) gives back what the deletion took — its place in `live_metarecords`, the counts and the parents (`KvTxn::restore_deleted`) — and the entries that stayed are right again. What they describe is in the record itself, which a deletion does not remove: it stays, with the rows it held, until a reindex frees it, and a read of a record is always sifted through `live_metarecords` (one read more per chunk at most: the transaction keeps the chunk, and the store keeps the chunks a read of the committed store looked up until the next write — a read of one record after another, as resolving the ancestors of the nodes an `osm` path finds, opens a read each, and paid that key on every one: 1 686 keys instead of 1 124 for the finder on 20 000 files, `tests/kv_cost.rs`). An id of `ids` outside `live_metarecords` is a deleted metarecord's if it has a record, a reserved one if not ([[Store tables]]). No search of the log is needed, and none can fail: a prune forgets nothing a restoration reads. A creation under a uuid the store knows restores it the same way, then removes its rows in the ordinary way, which unpicks what the indexes held of them; a revert of an older deletion restores, then changes the rows into the ones it wants. A deletion of many records still pending is given back for one operation by `mass_undelete_log`, the operation being the id's rank among the targets of its log entry.
* ''Every answer is sifted through `live_metarecords`.'' The query source intersects each bitmap it hands the evaluator with it, read once per query (`KvSource::sift`); a walk of the postings steps over the ids that are not in it. An entry is about one id, so a deleted id's entries cannot change what is said of another.
* ''An `index_raw_holder` entry names a record that is gone.'' It holds its metarecord's dense id, so listing a folder or resolving a path tests the id against `live_metarecords` without another read; a new position written on such a key replaces it.

What `live_metarecords` cannot sift is kept exact at the deletion: the counts above, and
`index_parents`. A metarecord that still has children that exist is taken the same short way: the `Writer` refuses to commit that (`check_forest_integrity`), and a write that deletes the folder before its files leaves, once they are gone too, nothing to unpick.

!! What it costs

* Each query reads `live_metarecords` (one key per 65 536 metarecords) and intersects its leaves with it.
* The leftovers take room, and slow the scans that cross them, until `mf repo reindex` drops them: it renumbers the live metarecords, drops the deleted ones' records and ids, and rebuilds the indexes from the rows ([[Checking and reindexing a repository]]). So [[Compacting a repository]] gives back the room of a large deletion only after a reindex.
* `mf repo check` holds the indexes to the rows ''on the ids of the metarecords that exist''; an id that was never given is still reported.

The tests are `tests/kv_delete.rs`: which tables a deletion writes
(`KvStore::last_write_keys`), and every kind of answer held to the oracle after
deletions, reverts and a reindex.

!! Deleting many records

A deletion of many records at once does less still: a pending write of kind `delete`
says that the records hold the before of the deletion until an idle fold copies it into
the log, and only `live_metarecords`, the type counts and the parents change
([[Mass writes in the store]], [[Mass operations in the log]]).
